HR:664: 3. **No absolute week numbers until the real scale is written.** A realm's span is 2–8 march-weeks under the per-world normalisation, so a fixed number of weeks in a rule is a trap. Until the real-distance scale is supplied, every world runs the relative reading, unchanged. The map must not label a site "two weeks away" in absolute terms on a world without the scale. Holds until the host's creation flow writes `kmS
HR:671: 10. **No invented fact: four kinds of mark, and only four.** A map lens may draw only: facts the host stores, drawn as stored; facts recomputed by the host's own exported function on the same snapshot, labelled so, never a re-implementation; map geometry used only to place a host fact (a territory fill, a "likely road", a site's centre), labelled when it is a guess; and the two owner-ruled map derivations, the sway a
HR:676: 15. **Weather slows travel, never cuts it.** Winter and storm season make travel slower, capped, never impossible. Host travel to a site is never closed by season; V14's own "not discoverable" (every entrance flooded deep or bricked) is the map's physics of the site, not a host travel rule (an inference). Always. (ENC-FL-15; `research/sf/fp-physical-integration.md` §2.7 row S2 (line 124; "WINTER TRAVEL — SLOW, NOT
HR:677: 16. **A port needs both geography and institutions.** The map may say a place could be a port; only the host's institutions make it one. A harbour encounter or coastal site may not claim a working port the host has not made. Always. (ENC-FL-16; `research/sf/fp-physical-integration.md` §2.4 row W4 (line 84); the owner's port rule; ports totality, route lifecycle Law 6)
